# water_falls.fmat — a waterfall's falling sheet (docs/terrain-paint/
# PHASE_W4.md). ground_edit places a sheet over every fall its rivers make,
# wearing this (its falls_material); put it on any mesh hung as a fall.
#
# `falls` switches the water shader to its falling-water path: streaks
# scrolling down the mesh's V (metres down the sheet) at falls_speed, the
# sheet glassy and see-through where it leaves the lip and white, aerated
# and opaque toward the foot (the vertex colour's red), its sides frayed
# (green). The rest are the water effect's own numbers, chosen for a sheet:
# no swell (a vertical mesh must not be displaced by the surface's waves),
# no flow map (the vertex colour is the sheet's data, not a current), no
# caustics or screen reflections; the colours are the river's, so the glassy
# part matches the water that went over the lip.
kind: surface
texture: water
doc: 'A waterfall''s falling sheet: streaks running down, white water at the lip and foot, see-through where it is glassy.'
effects:
  - effect: water
    falls: 1
    falls_speed: 4
    wave_amplitude: 0
    swell: 0
    flow_map: 0
    absorption: 0.7
    shallow_color: {r: 0.66, g: 0.78, b: 0.64}
    scatter_color: {r: 0.09, g: 0.145, b: 0.105}
    roughness: 0.2
    refraction: 0.5
    reflectivity: 0.34
    reflect_color: {r: 0.92, g: 0.96, b: 0.76}
    foam: 1
    detail_scale: 1.2
    detail_strength: 1.2
    caustics: 0
    ssr: 0
